window.__hvBdRegister("epictetus-discourses", {"key":"epictetus-discourses","sections":[{"i":0,"j":20,"loc":"Book I, chapters 1–2","title":"What is in our power; keeping one's character","summary":"Epictetus opens Book I by arguing that only the rational faculty examines itself and other faculties, and that the gods placed in our power only the right use of appearances. In a speech given to Zeus, the body is called clay finely tempered, and the faculty of desire and aversion is a small portion of the divine. Examples of Lateranus, Thrasea, Rufus and Agrippinus show calm in the face of death and exile. Chapter 2 holds that what counts as rational differs with each person's sense of his own worth; Agrippinus, Priscus Helvidius before Vespasian, and Epictetus himself refusing to shave illustrate choosing to be the purple thread in the toga.","ideas":["Only the use of appearances is truly in our power; body, property and kin are not","Chains and death touch the body, not the will","Each person must weigh at what price he sells himself","Not reaching Socrates' level is no reason to neglect oneself"],"terms":[{"t":"phantasiai (appearances)","d":"The impressions things make on the mind, whose right use is the one thing given wholly into our power."},{"t":"prosōpon (character, role)","d":"The particular worth and role a person recognizes in himself and must weigh in deciding what to do."}]},{"i":21,"j":33,"loc":"Book I, chapters 3–5","title":"Kinship with God, progress, against the Academics","summary":"Chapter 3 argues that if we grasp that God is father of gods and men, we will think nothing mean of ourselves; those who lean toward the flesh become like wolves, lions or foxes. Chapter 4 defines progress not as reading much Chrysippus but as exercising desire, aversion, pursuit and assent so as never to be disappointed or deceived; the effect, not the treatise, is what counts, as with an athlete's shoulders. Tragedy is called the display of the passions of men who value externals, and Chrysippus is praised as a benefactor greater than Triptolemus. Chapter 5 says there is no arguing with a man hardened against evident truth, whose sense of shame is deadened.","ideas":["Human beings share body with animals and reason with the gods","True progress lies in one's use of will, not in book learning","Tragedy shows men who value externals","Denying the evident is a deadening of shame, not strength"],"terms":[{"t":"prokopē (progress)","d":"Advance toward virtue, shown in tranquillity and in right desire, aversion and assent."},{"t":"prohairesis (will)","d":"The faculty of choice which the one making progress turns to and trains to be free and unimpeded."}]},{"i":34,"j":38,"loc":"Book I, chapter 6","title":"Providence seen in the order of things","summary":"Epictetus argues that a grateful person who sees how things fit together, colours with sight and light, male with female, will recognize a maker in the world. Animals only use appearances, but humans were brought in as spectators and interpreters of God's works, and must not end where the animals end. People travel to Olympia to see Phidias' statue yet neglect the works before them, enduring heat and crowds for that spectacle. Hercules' labours show that hardships exist to exercise the powers God has given free and unimpeded, so complaining against God is meanness of spirit and impiety.","ideas":["The fitting together of things argues for a designer","Man is meant to be a spectator and interpreter of God's works","Difficulties exist to exercise our powers, as the beasts did Hercules","Our faculties are given wholly into our own power"],"terms":[{"t":"pronoia (providence)","d":"The divine care seen in the ordered structure of the world and of human faculties."}]},{"i":39,"j":47,"loc":"Book I, chapters 7–8","title":"Logic and its dangers for the untrained","summary":"Chapter 7 argues that handling sophistical and hypothetical arguments belongs to the duties of life, since a good man must be able to tell true from false and see what follows from what he has granted. Premises that change meaning in the course of an argument release us from their conclusions, much as a repaid loan ends a debt. Epictetus recalls Rufus rebuking him for missing a step in a syllogism: careless use of appearances is itself a real fault. Chapter 8 warns that skill in argument and persuasion, in the uninstructed, breeds conceit, and that the good of man is a certain disposition of the will toward appearances, not Plato's looks or eloquence.","ideas":["Logical skill is like the skill of telling genuine coins from false","One must abide by premises only while they stay as granted","Rhetorical power puffs up the untrained","Do not confuse what is accidental to a philosopher with philosophy"],"terms":[{"t":"enthymēma (enthymeme)","d":"An imperfect or abbreviated syllogism, whose forms the philosopher should be able to vary."},{"t":"lēmmata (premises)","d":"The assumptions granted in argument, binding only so long as they remain unchanged."}]},{"i":48,"j":58,"loc":"Book I, chapters 9–10","title":"Citizen of the world; against seeking office","summary":"Chapter 9 draws the consequences of kinship with God: one should call oneself a citizen of the world and a son of God and fear nothing among men. Epictetus imagines youths begging to leave the body and return to their kin, and answers that they must wait for God's signal and keep their post, as Socrates refused to abandon his. Stories follow of a ruined man who wanted help, not pity, and of Musonius Rufus testing Epictetus. Chapter 10 tells of an old acquaintance who vowed to live quietly but plunged back into business at Caesar's letters, and urges students to be as zealous about their own work as officials are about grain.","ideas":["The greatest community is that of God and men","The door is open, but one should wait for God's signal","Help from oneself makes favours from others superfluous","Officials' busyness shames students' idleness"],"terms":[{"t":"kosmios (citizen of the world)","d":"Epictetus' answer, following Socrates, to the question of what country one belongs to."}]},{"i":59,"j":68,"loc":"Book I, chapters 11–12","title":"Natural affection and contentment with providence","summary":"In chapter 11 a magistrate admits he fled home while his daughter was sick; through questioning Epictetus shows this was neither reasonable nor truly affectionate, since mother, nurse and tutor would then have had to flee too. The cause of every act is our own opinion and will, not death or the child, so it is opinions that must be examined. Chapter 12 lists five views about the gods, endorses the view that they watch over everything, and defines being instructed as learning to wish that things happen as they do. Solitude should be called freedom, crowds a festival, and the only responsibility the gods gave us is the proper use of appearances.","ideas":["What most people do is not thereby natural or right","Our opinions, not events, cause our actions","Freedom is willing things as the disposer arranged them","The discontented are already in the prison of their unwillingness"],"terms":[{"t":"dogmata (opinions, judgments)","d":"The beliefs that are the real cause of what we do or refrain from doing."},{"t":"kritērion (criterion)","d":"The standard by which things according to nature are distinguished from things contrary to it."}]},{"i":69,"j":82,"loc":"Book I, chapters 13–16","title":"Divine oversight, philosophy's promise, providence","summary":"Chapter 13 says eating justly and bearing with a careless slave, a brother sprung from Zeus, pleases the gods. Chapter 14 argues that if plants obey the seasons and souls are portions of God, God perceives all things, and he has given each man a sleepless Daemon as guardian, to whom one should swear an oath like a soldier's. Chapter 15 tells a man that philosophy promises nothing external, only to keep his own governing part according to nature, and that such fruit ripens slowly like a fig. Chapter 16 praises providence in animals' ready-made provisions and the beard as a sign of sex, and Epictetus, a lame old man, declares his work is to sing hymns to God.","ideas":["Slaves are kinsmen and offspring of Zeus","God and one's Daemon are within, so no one is ever alone","Philosophy's material is one's own life, not another's anger","A rational creature's task is to praise God"],"terms":[{"t":"daimōn (guardian spirit)","d":"The guardian Zeus has placed beside each person, who never sleeps and is never deceived."},{"t":"hēgemonikon (governing part)","d":"The ruling faculty that philosophy promises to keep conformable to nature."}]},{"i":83,"j":91,"loc":"Book I, chapters 17–18","title":"Logic necessary; no anger at wrongdoers","summary":"Chapter 17 argues that reason must analyse itself, so logic comes first as the examination of the measure comes before measuring grain; Antisthenes and Socrates also began with names. Chrysippus matters only as an interpreter of the will of nature, as a diviner matters for the signs; the 'diviner' reads in the viscera that the will is by nature free from compulsion, since only opinion compels. Chapter 18 holds that wrongdoers are mistaken about good and evil and deserve pity, not anger. We are angry only because we value what they take; Epictetus tells of his stolen iron lamp, and describes the invincible man as one undisturbed by anything outside the will.","ideas":["Logic is the measure that tests everything else","No one can compel assent; will is overcome only by will","Thieves share our false valuation of externals","We only lose what we have"],"terms":[{"t":"ekklisis (aversion)","d":"The turning away from something, which only another aversion or desire can overcome."}]},{"i":92,"j":103,"loc":"Book I, chapters 19–21","title":"Tyrants, self-contemplating reason, wanting admiration","summary":"Chapter 19 argues that a tyrant can give no one unhindered desire, and is attended to only as one tends a platter or an ass; he masters the body, not the will set free by Zeus. Self-regard is natural, yet Zeus made rational creatures such that they gain their own good only by serving the common good. Anecdotes of Felicion the shoemaker, a new tribune and a seeker of Augustus' priesthood show people valuing what lies outside the will. Chapter 20 explains that reason, being a system of appearances, contemplates itself and must test appearances as an assayer tests coins; chapter 21 mocks the would-be philosopher who wants madmen to admire him.","ideas":["Opinions, not tyrants or guards, frighten people","Serving oneself rightly means contributing to the common good","The philosopher's first work is to test appearances","Being admired by the crowd is being admired by madmen"],"terms":[{"t":"oikeiōsis (attachment to self)","d":"The principle, common to all animals, of regard for oneself and one's own interest."}]},{"i":104,"j":115,"loc":"Book I, chapters 22–24","title":"Preconceptions, Epicurus, and the use of difficulties","summary":"Chapter 22 says all share the same preconceptions of good and justice; disputes, like Agamemnon and Achilles over Chryseis, arise in applying them to cases. Education is learning to apply them and to distinguish what is in our power; placing the good in externals breeds theft, war and hatred of Zeus. Chapter 23 argues that Epicurus, though he sees we are social, contradicts nature by advising against raising children. Chapter 24 treats difficulties as God matching us with a rough wrestling partner, contrasts a cowardly scout with Diogenes, who reports that death is no evil, and calls possessions beds in an inn, adding that the door is open.","ideas":["Conflict comes from misapplying shared preconceptions","Placing good in externals sets people against each other and the gods","Even sheep and wolves do not desert their young","Hardships reveal what a person is"],"terms":[{"t":"prolēpsis (praecognition, preconception)","d":"A natural general notion, such as that the good is to be pursued, common to all people."}]},{"i":116,"j":126,"loc":"Book I, chapters 25–26","title":"Playing the game; the law of life","summary":"Chapter 25 argues that Zeus has already given the only directions needed: keep what is your own and do not desire what belongs to others. Life is like the Saturnalia game or a hypothesis granted in argument; one plays along while it is fitting, and when the smoke grows too thick the door is open, as Demetrius told Nero. It is our opinions that squeeze us, and a reviled man who listens like a stone gives the reviler nothing. Chapter 26 sets the law of life, acting according to nature, above logical rules, notes that theory is easier than practice, and says philosophy begins with perceiving the weakness of one's ruling faculty.","ideas":["Fidelity and shame are one's own and cannot be taken","We put ourselves in straits through our opinions","Wrongdoing comes from ignorance, which anger cannot teach away","Philosophy begins with knowing one's own weakness"],"terms":[]},{"i":127,"j":140,"loc":"Book I, chapters 27–28","title":"Remedies against appearances; why not be angry","summary":"Chapter 27 lists four ways appearances present themselves and prescribes remedies: reason against sophistry, contrary habit against habit, and clear preconceptions against deceiving appearances. Death cannot be escaped, but fear of it can, and piety survives only where it coincides with one's interest; against the Pyrrhonists, Epictetus points out that they never mistake a mill for a bath. Chapter 28 holds that no one assents to what seems false or chooses what seems harmful; Medea was deceived and deserves pity. The Iliad and the tragedies spring from appearances, and the real ruin of Alexander or Achilles was the loss of modesty and fidelity, not the burning of what are only storks' nests.","ideas":["Against habit, train the contrary habit","Every soul is deprived of truth unwillingly","Men differ from storks in understanding, fidelity and modesty","Following every appearance is what madmen do"],"terms":[{"t":"sunkatathesis (assent)","d":"Agreement to an appearance as true, which the mind cannot give to what seems false."}]},{"i":141,"j":157,"loc":"Book I, chapters 29–30","title":"Constancy before tyrants and great men","summary":"Chapter 29 states that the good and bad are kinds of will, externals are its material, and only will can conquer will; ten men are stronger only in chaining and killing. Socrates' body was dragged to prison, but, as he said, Anytus and Melitus could kill and not harm him. Difficulties are the test of one's schooling, like a complex syllogism or a worthy opponent, and one should appear as a witness summoned by God; with the ignorant, as Socrates with the weeping jailor, one should be indulgent. Chapter 30 imagines God asking a student going in to a great man what he said in school about exile and death, and finding him unchanged.","ideas":["Opinion is conquered only by itself","A judgment by the uninstructed condemns no one","Students should become living examples, not cite the ancients","Masters are formidable only through death, pain and pleasure"],"terms":[{"t":"adiaphora (things indifferent)","d":"Things outside the will, such as exile, bonds and death, which are neither good nor bad."}]},{"i":158,"j":167,"loc":"Book II, chapter 1","title":"Confidence and caution are compatible","summary":"Book II opens by defending the paradox that one can be both cautious and confident: cautious about what depends on the will, confident about what does not. People act like deer that flee the feathers into the nets, fearing death and pain while being reckless in their judgments. Socrates called such terrors masks that frighten children; turned round, death and pain do not bite. Only the educated are free, since freedom is living as one chooses, free from fear and sorrow, and a slave freed before the praetor is not thereby free; a student should show this in practice, not in little dissertations.","ideas":["Be confident toward externals, cautious toward the will","Fear of death, not death, is what is formidable","Only the educated are truly free","Show philosophy by facing death and prison, not by display"],"terms":[{"t":"ataraxia (freedom from perturbation)","d":"The release from fear and disturbance that is the fruit of right opinions."}]},{"i":168,"j":178,"loc":"Book II, chapters 2–4","title":"Tranquillity in court; recommendations; an adulterer","summary":"Chapter 2 advises someone going to trial that if he wishes to keep only his will, he is secure; Socrates said his whole life had been preparation. If he wants to keep externals, he must wholly submit and plead, not waver between free and slave; Heraclitus at Rhodes and Socrates are contrasted. Chapter 3 recalls Diogenes' answer that a man recommends himself to anyone able to test him, like a drachma. In chapter 4 Epictetus tells a man of letters once caught in adultery that he has destroyed fidelity, the mark of a man, and so has no place left, any more than a useless utensil.","ideas":["One must be fully free or fully a slave, not both","General principles prepare one for any case, as literacy does","Good men are known only to those skilled in judging","Things common by nature become one's own once distributed"],"terms":[{"t":"pistis (fidelity)","d":"The trustworthiness for which man is formed, whose destruction overturns friendship and community."}]},{"i":179,"j":192,"loc":"Book II, chapters 5–7","title":"Care without anxiety; indifference; divination","summary":"Chapter 5 argues that materials are indifferent but their use is not, like dice or a ball in play; Socrates played skilfully with life and death at his trial. A man considered as part of a whole, like a foot in the body, must sometimes suffer sickness or early death for the whole. Chapter 6 quotes Chrysippus on moving toward what is fated, compares men to ears of corn that must be reaped, and calls exile and danger empty names. Chapter 7 says the inner diviner already tells us good and evil, so cowardice makes us flatter diviners; we should consult God as a guide without desire or aversion.","ideas":["Act carefully because use matters, calmly because the material is indifferent","A man cut off from others is no longer a man","All roads to Hades are equal","Duty to a friend needs no diviner"],"terms":[{"t":"hulē (material)","d":"The external things on which a person exercises skill, valued not for themselves but for their use."},{"t":"mantikē (divination)","d":"The reading of signs about outcomes, which cannot tell what is good or evil."}]},{"i":193,"j":200,"loc":"Book II, chapters 8–9","title":"The good, the god within, and man's promise","summary":"Epictetus argues that since God and the good are both beneficial, the good must lie where God's nature lies: in intelligence, knowledge and right reason, which only rational animals can understand. Man is a portion separated from the deity and carries a god within, so he should be ashamed to pollute it, as a statue by Phidias would honour its maker. In chapter 9 he asks what a man's nature promises: acting like a sheep or a wild beast loses the man, and each character is preserved or destroyed by corresponding acts. He ends by rebuking students who recite Stoic doctrine but act otherwise, taking up a philosopher's profession while failing even at being a man.","ideas":["The good is sought in the rational animal, where God's nature is","Each person carries a god within and is entrusted to himself","Character is kept or lost by corresponding acts","Doctrine must be digested into practice, not stored"],"terms":[{"t":"ousia (nature, substance)","d":"The essential being of the good, which Epictetus locates in reason."},{"t":"chrēsis phantasiōn (use of appearances)","d":"The faculty animals share, while only rational beings understand how they use it."}]},{"i":201,"j":208,"loc":"Book II, chapters 10–11","title":"Duties from names; the beginning of philosophy","summary":"Epictetus shows how roles such as man, citizen of the world, son, brother, senator, youth or old man each mark out proper duties. Losing modesty, fidelity or gentleness is real damage, worse than losing money, and to harm someone who harmed you is to injure your own will. In chapter 11 he says philosophy begins with awareness of one's weakness: all have innate preconceptions of good and evil but quarrel in applying them, so mere seeming cannot be the measure. Philosophy is the search for a rule, like a balance for weights, by which pleasure, for instance, is tested and rejected as insecure.","ideas":["Each name or role implies its own duties","Harm lies in the will, not in body or property","People disagree in fitting shared preconceptions to cases","Philosophy seeks and applies a rule above seeming"],"terms":[{"t":"prolēpsis (preconception)","d":"A natural notion of good, just and the like that all bring into the world."},{"t":"oiēsis (self-conceit)","d":"The false belief that one already applies preconceptions correctly."},{"t":"kanōn (rule)","d":"The standard by which appearances are tested, like a balance or a carpenter's square."}]},{"i":209,"j":218,"loc":"Book II, chapters 12–13","title":"Discussion like Socrates; the sources of anxiety","summary":"Epictetus says Stoics know the rules of disputation but cannot use them with an unlearned man, whom they mock instead of guiding. Socrates drew conclusions from his adversary's own notions, needed no other witness, and never grew angry, though such questioning is now dangerous at Rome, as Epictetus found himself. In chapter 13 he traces anxiety to wanting what is not in one's power, like a lute player seeking applause. Zeno was calm before Antigonus, while Antigonus was anxious; a man fearing a powerful listener should either practise speaking as Crates and Diogenes did or admit his masters.","ideas":["Guide the mistaken person rather than abuse him","Socrates made his opponent his only witness","Anxiety comes from desiring externals","Practised skill gives confidence"],"terms":[{"t":"elenchus-style questioning","d":"Leading an adversary through his own natural notions until he sees his contradiction."}]},{"i":219,"j":226,"loc":"Book II, chapters 14–15","title":"Instruction to Naso; against obstinate resolve","summary":"Speaking to the Roman Naso, Epictetus says the philosopher's work is to fit his wish to events and maintain his relations; it requires learning that God exists and provides, and imitating the gods' fidelity, freedom and beneficence. He tells Naso he does not understand even names and does not know what God, man, good or bad are, comparing himself to a mirror or a physician. Life is like a fair where most care only for fodder and a few ask who governs the world. Chapter 15 warns that firmness is good only when the decision is sound, recounting a companion who resolved to starve himself and was barely persuaded to stop.","ideas":["The philosopher harmonises his will with what happens","Man must imitate the divine nature","The few at life's fair inquire into its governor","Holding to an unsound decision is madness, not firmness"],"terms":[{"t":"boulēsis (wish)","d":"The will that the philosopher adapts to the course of events."},{"t":"tonos (tone, tension)","d":"Healthy firmness of mind, contrasted with the frenzied tone of the obstinate."}]},{"i":227,"j":235,"loc":"Book II, chapter 16","title":"Failing to practise our views of good and evil","summary":"Epictetus asks why students who know that good and evil lie in the will never rehearse this outside school, so that like the rhetorician or lute player they still fear praise and blame. We are disturbed not by the sea or an earthquake but by our opinions, and we cling to places and people like children to a nurse. The divine law is to keep what is ours, use what is given and give it up readily with thanks. He urges the student to look up to God and accept any condition, and to purge himself of passions as Hercules and Theseus purged the world of monsters.","ideas":["Doctrines must be practised against real appearances","Opinions, not things, weigh on us","Return what is taken away with thanks","Purge sadness, fear and desire by looking to God"],"terms":[{"t":"prohairesis (will)","d":"The faculty of choice, the sole seat of good and evil."},{"t":"apatheia / ataraxia (tranquillity)","d":"Freedom from perturbation sought by surrendering externals to God."}]},{"i":236,"j":244,"loc":"Book II, chapter 17","title":"Adapting preconceptions to particular cases","summary":"The first task of a philosopher is to throw away self-conceit, since no one learns what he thinks he knows. Against Theopompus, Epictetus says we have preconceptions of good and just, as of health, but cannot apply them without analysis, which is why physicians and moralists disagree. Unhappiness proves faulty application, as Medea's murder of her children shows a soul that sought its wishes from outside. He calls for a student who will first master desire and aversion, then duties, then security of assent, instead of studying Chrysippus on the Liar.","ideas":["Self-conceit blocks learning","Disagreement shows preconceptions are misapplied","Desire only what God wills and you will not be thwarted","The three topics come in order: desire, duty, assent"],"terms":[{"t":"prolēpsis (preconception)","d":"A shared natural notion that must be analysed before it is applied."},{"t":"topos (field of study)","d":"One of the three areas of training: desire, impulse and duty, assent."}]},{"i":245,"j":259,"loc":"Book II, chapters 18–19","title":"Fighting appearances; philosophy only in words","summary":"Every habit grows by its acts, so each outburst of anger or lust strengthens the vice; Epictetus advises counting the days without anger and making a sacrifice after thirty. Against a seductive appearance one should pause, test it, set a noble image against it, and recall Socrates' victory over himself with Alcibiades; this is the true athlete's contest. Chapter 19 describes the Master Argument of Diodorus and the rival Stoic positions, then mocks reciting such doctrines like a grammarian's stories. Students who tremble in a storm or before Caesar are Epicureans in practice; Epictetus asks to be shown even one Stoic in the making.","ideas":["Acts feed or starve habits","Test an appearance before letting it carry you off","Learning doctrines is not holding them","A Stoic is happy in sickness, danger and exile"],"terms":[{"t":"kyrieuōn logos (Master Argument)","d":"Diodorus' trilemma about past truth, possibility and impossibility."},{"t":"phantasia (appearance)","d":"An impression on the mind that must be examined before assent."}]},{"i":260,"j":266,"loc":"Book II, chapter 20","title":"Against the Epicureans and Academics","summary":"Epictetus argues that evident truths are used even by those who deny them: the Academics who say nothing can be known still try to teach it. Epicurus denies natural fellowship among men yet labours to write books for others, driven by the nature he denies. Philosophers who teach that the gods do not exist and justice is nothing undermine states, yet marry, hold priesthoods and consult oracles. Those who doubt their senses still bring food to their mouths, and Epictetus judges them too blind to their own evils to be reasoned with.","ideas":["Denying evident truths requires using them","Epicurus' own effort refutes his denial of fellowship","Impious doctrines corrupt the young and the city","Sceptics live by the senses they reject"],"terms":[{"t":"koinōnia (natural fellowship)","d":"The bond among rational beings that Epicurus denies and Epictetus defends."}]},{"i":267,"j":282,"loc":"Book II, chapters 21–22","title":"Inconsistency, and the nature of friendship","summary":"People admit faults they think involuntary, such as timidity or compassion, but never folly or injustice; Epictetus urges watching whether one is such a person, and blames students who bring home troubles to school and then call it useless. In chapter 22 he argues that only the wise can love, since love follows what one judges good. Every animal clings to its own interest, so brothers, parents and friends turn enemies over an estate, a woman or power, as with Eteocles and Polynices or Admetus' father. Friendship is secure only where one's interest is placed in the will, with fidelity and modesty.","ideas":["Confusion about good and evil breeds inconsistency","Theorems help only the tranquil mind","Where a man places his interest decides his friendships","Friendship exists only where good is placed in the will"],"terms":[{"t":"sympheron (interest, advantage)","d":"What every animal clings to; it must be placed in the will for loyalty to hold."}]},{"i":283,"j":293,"loc":"Book II, chapter 23","title":"On the faculty of speaking and the will","summary":"Epictetus says denying a faculty of expression is impious and timid, since sight, hearing and speech are gifts of God. Yet the will is the highest faculty: it judges the others, decides when to use them, and can be hindered only by itself, so it alone is virtue or vice. He rebukes Epicurus for calling the flesh best when it was his will that wrote his books. Eloquence and theorems are inns on the road home, not the destination, and he warns students not to linger among them as among Sirens.","ideas":["Speech and the senses are real gifts to be valued","The will uses and judges every other faculty","Each thing should be given its due worth","Style and theorems are inns, not home"],"terms":[{"t":"prohairesis (will)","d":"The ruling faculty that alone can hinder itself and alone is good or bad."},{"t":"pandokeion (inn)","d":"Image for studies one should pass through without staying."}]},{"i":294,"j":304,"loc":"Book II, chapters 24–26","title":"Skilled hearing, why logic is needed, error","summary":"To a man who complains that Epictetus never speaks to him, Epictetus replies that hearing needs skill as speaking does, and a hearer who knows nothing of man, nature or demonstration gives the speaker nothing to respond to. Ignorance of what is profitable caused the quarrel of Agamemnon and Achilles, and wealth or beauty does not help. Chapter 25 proves that logic is necessary, since without it one cannot judge whether a proof of that is a cheat. Chapter 26 says every error contains a contradiction, because the wrongdoer does not do what he wishes, and a soul shown its contradiction must withdraw from it, as Socrates knew.","ideas":["A listener must stir the speaker to speak","Ignorance of the profitable causes quarrels","Logic is needed even to question logic","Showing a contradiction moves the rational soul"],"terms":[{"t":"machē (contradiction)","d":"The conflict between what the erring person wishes and what he does."}]},{"i":305,"j":312,"loc":"Book III, chapter 1","title":"Of finery in dress","summary":"Book III opens with a young rhetorician who arrives with dressed hair and plucked body. Epictetus argues that each creature is beautiful by the excellence of its own nature, so a man is beautiful through justice, moderation and temperance. He feels bound to speak, as Apollo spoke to Laius and Socrates questioned everyone, since silence would be cruelty. Man's peculiar possession is the rational faculty; the youth should adorn his will, leave his body as nature made it, and take the warning as sent by a god.","ideas":["Beauty is excellence according to one's nature","Human beauty lies in virtue","A teacher must speak even if unheeded","You are not flesh and hair but will"],"terms":[{"t":"aretē (excellence)","d":"The quality that makes each creature beautiful in its own kind."},{"t":"prohairesis (will)","d":"What a man really is, and what he should beautify."}]},{"i":313,"j":323,"loc":"Book III, chapters 2–3","title":"The three fields of training; the rule","summary":"Epictetus sets out three fields of exercise: desire and aversion, which govern the passions and come first; impulses and duties, maintaining one's relations as son, father and citizen; and assent, which secures the other two. Current philosophers neglect the first two for sophisms while trembling at what others say about them. In chapter 3 he says the good man's material is his ruling faculty, and the soul is drawn to the apparent good as a merchant accepts Caesar's coin. One should test every sight each morning by asking whether it depends on the will, since all distress is opinion.","ideas":["Desire, duty and assent are trained in that order","Logic suits only those already free of passion","The soul cannot reject a clear appearance of good","Ask of each impression whether it lies in the will"],"terms":[{"t":"pathos (passion, affect)","d":"Disturbance arising from frustrated desire or unavoided aversion."},{"t":"synkatathesis (assent)","d":"Agreement to an appearance, the third field of training."},{"t":"hēgemonikon (ruling faculty)","d":"The mind, which is the wise man's proper material."}]},{"i":324,"j":334,"loc":"Book III, chapters 4–6","title":"Theatre partisanship, sickness, sayings","summary":"Epictetus tells the governor of Epirus, blamed for cheering an actor, that he set the example the crowd followed and should wish the winner to be whoever wins. In chapter 5 he answers a sick pupil who wants to go home: one should wish to be found by death while tending the will, and he gives a prayer of thanks to God for life. Socrates is cited as delighting daily in growing better, not in fine words. Chapter 6 gathers sayings: past progress was in keeping the ruling faculty natural, the good man is invincible, common sense is defined, and Rufus tested pupils by discouraging them.","ideas":["Leaders set the pattern for the crowd","Meet death while caring for one's will","Progress follows what reason is cultivated for","The good man contends only where he is stronger"],"terms":[{"t":"koinos nous (common sense)","d":"The ordinary notions all undistorted minds share."}]},{"i":335,"j":342,"loc":"Book III, chapters 7–8","title":"Against an Epicurean official; testing appearances","summary":"Epictetus questions an Epicurean administrator of the free cities: if soul is better than flesh, its good should be in the will, yet Epicurean doctrine makes bodily pleasure the good. He ironically draws out that on this view a judge should steal and seduce if undetected, and an Epicurean city could not marry, raise children or govern. True governing treats men as rational beings, as Socrates did, not by prison and rods. Chapter 8 prescribes daily drill against appearances, labelling each event as within or beyond the will, and ends with Italicus' complaint about Epictetus.","ideas":["Epicurean pleasure contradicts the soul's superiority","Epicurean doctrine would dissolve the state","Rule rational beings by showing them what profits","Treat each event as a question about the will"],"terms":[{"t":"ta proēgoumena (principal things)","d":"Duties such as public life, marriage and piety, to which pleasure should be subordinate."}]},{"i":343,"j":350,"loc":"Book III, chapters 9–10","title":"A suitor bound for Rome; bearing sickness","summary":"To a man sailing to Rome over a suit about his rank, Epictetus says he has no rule for winning cases, only that right opinions mean faring well, since opinion causes all action. The man has never travelled to examine his own opinions; a true meeting with a philosopher means exchanging and correcting them. Epictetus calls himself richer for lacking needs, like a child who must drop figs to free his hand from a jar. Chapter 10 says to keep the right opinions ready, as in the Pythagorean verses on nightly review, and to bear fever well: blame no one, neither fear nor flatter the physician, and follow events.","ideas":["Opinion is the cause of every action","Meeting a philosopher means examining opinions","Few desires bring sufficiency","Fever is a test of the philosopher, not a break from philosophy"],"terms":[{"t":"dogma (opinion, judgement)","d":"The belief that drives each act and decides whether one fares well."},{"t":"theōrēma (rule, theorem)","d":"A philosophical principle, aimed at keeping the ruling faculty natural."}]},{"i":351,"j":364,"loc":"Book III, chapters 11–13","title":"Penalties, right training, and being alone","summary":"Epictetus says that those who place good or evil outside the will are punished by envy, grief and fear, and that Zeus watches over every family relation. On exercise, he rejects showy feats like rope-walking and says training should aim at desire and aversion that never fail, then at right impulses to act, and finally at testing appearances before assenting to them. On solitude, he separates being alone from being helpless: like Zeus at the conflagration, a man should be able to keep himself company and examine his own state. Caesar can give peace from war, but only philosophy gives peace from grief, fear and death, which is only a return to the elements. He ends by warning beginners to practise abstinence and to help others by example, not by lecturing.","ideas":["Exercise should train desire, aversion and assent, not win admiration","The three topics: desire and aversion, impulse to act, and assent","Solitude means helplessness, not merely being without company","The peace God gives through reason, unlike Caesar's peace, covers grief and death"],"terms":[{"t":"askēsis (exercise, training)","d":"Deliberate practice that sets a contrary habit against the habit of desiring things outside the will."},{"t":"erēmia (solitude)","d":"The state of a man left without help, which Epictetus separates from simply being alone."}]},{"i":365,"j":377,"loc":"Book III, chapters 14–16","title":"Self-examination, forethought, and dangerous company","summary":"A set of short sayings tells the student to walk and talk alone, not to boast of drinking water, and to remove arrogance by refutation, as Socrates did, and despair by inquiry. Rank and a father's consulship do not make one man superior to another; modesty, fidelity and justice do. Chapter 15 says to weigh what comes before and after any undertaking, using the Olympic athlete's hardships as the model, and warns against trying philosophy, rhetoric and public office in turn like a child or an ape. Chapter 16 warns beginners that keeping company with ordinary people will melt their school principles like wax in the sun, because the others speak from settled opinions. They should avoid such company until their principles are firm.","ideas":["Arrogance is cured by refutation, despair by showing that the thing is possible","Count the cost of philosophy before taking it up, as an athlete counts the cost of training","A man must be one thing, either a philosopher or one of the crowd","Quenched and burning charcoal: company either corrupts you or is changed by you"],"terms":[{"t":"oiēsis (arrogance, self-conceit)","d":"The opinion that one lacks nothing, which Epictetus says Socrates removed by refutation."},{"t":"dogma (opinion, principle)","d":"A conviction actually held, which gives even foolish talk its force over principles only spoken."}]},{"i":378,"j":391,"loc":"Book III, chapters 17–21","title":"Providence, bad news, and would-be sophists","summary":"Epictetus defends Providence: the unjust man gains money because he flatters and has no shame, while the honest man keeps the better thing, his modesty, and a wife's ill treatment is no evil except through added opinion. Bad news never concerns the will; even an unjust verdict, as in Socrates' case, is the judge's evil, not yours. The ordinary man blames others for his misfortune, the philosopher blames only himself, and Epictetus traces the habit back to nurses who beat the stone a child tripped on. Like the rod of Hermes, a right opinion turns sickness, death and abuse to profit, as Menoeceus gained by dying. Finally he attacks those who rush to teach bare theorems and expound Chrysippus without having digested them, comparing them to people who reveal the Eleusinian mysteries without the rites.","ideas":["The superior gains in the very thing in which he is superior","No report can touch the will, so no news is disturbing","Any external thing can be made a benefit, like gold under Hermes' rod","Teaching philosophy needs fitness and God's call, not memorised words"],"terms":[{"t":"pronoia (providence)","d":"The ordering of the world by reason, which Epictetus says gives the better things to the better men."},{"t":"idiōtēs (common person)","d":"The untrained man, who blames others where the philosopher blames himself."},{"t":"theōrēmata (theorems)","d":"Philosophical doctrines that must be digested and shown in conduct before they are taught."}]},{"i":392,"j":408,"loc":"Book III, chapter 22 (first part)","title":"The true Cynic, messenger and spy","summary":"Asked by a pupil what a Cynic should be, Epictetus warns that whoever attempts the calling without God is hateful to God, and that it is not a matter of a cloak, a wallet and abuse of passers-by. The Cynic must give up desire, anger and envy, use modesty as his only door and darkness, and treat his understanding as his material. He is a messenger and spy sent from Zeus to tell men that happiness is not in body, wealth, office or royal power, as Agamemnon's sleepless distress in the Iliad shows. Through question and answer Epictetus finds the only free thing in us to be assent, desire and impulse. The section ends with the Cynic's own words on living without house or city yet free, and a warning that the calling is an Olympic contest.","ideas":["The Cynic's calling is appointed by God, like Agamemnon's or Achilles' role","The Cynic is a messenger and spy reporting where good and evil lie","Agamemnon's anguish shows that royal power does not give happiness","Assent and desire are what is naturally free in us"],"terms":[{"t":"kynismos (Cynism)","d":"The Cynic way of life, presented as a divine mission and not as a costume."},{"t":"aidōs (modesty, sense of shame)","d":"The Cynic's protection, standing in for the walls and doors others hide behind."},{"t":"kataskopos (spy, scout)","d":"The Cynic's role as one who examines what is good and evil for men and reports truly."}]},{"i":409,"j":428,"loc":"Book III, chapter 22 (second part)","title":"Cynic friendship, marriage, politics, and endurance","summary":"Answering the young man's questions, Epictetus says a sick Cynic hardly has a friend worthy of him, and that in the present state of the world, like an army in battle order, the Cynic should not marry, since household duties would keep him from his work as God's minister. Crates' marriage was an exception, born of love for a woman who was another Crates. The Cynic serves society more than parents do, as father of all men, and his 'politics' is to speak with everyone about happiness and freedom. He needs a healthy body, wit like Diogenes', a ruling faculty purer than the sun, and conscience in place of guards, so that his rebukes are not meddling. Epictetus ends by urging the pupil to put off the decision and look to his own fitness, as Hector knew his.","ideas":["Marriage is unsuited to the Cynic in a world like an army in the field","The Cynic is father of all and governs the largest commonwealth","Conscience gives the Cynic his right to reprove others","His body may be struck, but his will cannot be taken"],"terms":[{"t":"to syneidos (conscience)","d":"The Cynic's awareness of his own purity, which gives him the authority guards give a king."},{"t":"hēgemonikon (ruling faculty)","d":"The governing part of the soul, which in the Cynic must be purer than the sun."}]},{"i":429,"j":435,"loc":"Book III, chapter 23","title":"Against lecturing for applause","summary":"Epictetus tells those who read and discourse for display to decide first what they wish to be, since every activity needs the right end. He exposes the lecturer who sulks at a cool audience and fishes for praise of his passage on Pan and the nymphs, and who flatters a senator's son because he admires him. Such hearers learn how to talk, not how to live, and the speaker who counts his audience has misread Socrates, who said Anytus and Melitus could kill him but not harm him. A philosopher draws hearers as the sun or food does; the school is a surgery that one should leave in pain, not pleasure. He grants the hortatory, refutative and didactic styles but rejects a fourth style of display.","ideas":["Every activity must be referred to its proper end","Praise-seeking shows that the speaker's desire is not according to nature","The philosopher's school is a surgery, not a show","Exhortation shows hearers they seek happiness in the wrong place"],"terms":[{"t":"protreptikos charactēr (hortatory style)","d":"Speech that shows hearers the conflict between what they want and where they seek it."},{"t":"epideiktikos (display)","d":"Speaking to be admired, which Epictetus refuses to count as a philosophical style."}]},{"i":436,"j":443,"loc":"Book III, chapter 24 (first part)","title":"Partings, the world-city, and Hercules","summary":"Epictetus argues that another person's unhappiness need not be ours: God made men to be happy and gave them what is their own. Grieving over a friend who has gone abroad, or over familiar places, comes from forgetting that people are mortal and must part. He recalls the philosophers' teaching that the world is one city of gods and men, in which some things must give way to others. Hercules travelled the world, made friends everywhere, and left his children without grief because he knew Zeus is father of all. Ulysses' weeping, if real, would show him unhappy and so not good, and a mother's grief is her own to stop, not ours to cure by every means.","ideas":["Each man's unhappiness is his own fault","The world is one city, and change and separation belong to its order","Hercules lived happily everywhere because Zeus was his father","To demand what is impossible is to fight against God"],"terms":[{"t":"theomachein (fighting against God)","d":"Resisting the order of the universe by wanting what is not one's own."}]},{"i":444,"j":447,"loc":"Book III, chapter 24 (second part)","title":"Life as a campaign; how to love freely","summary":"Epictetus calls life a campaign in which each soldier must keep his post, and charges the homesick student with speaking like an Epicurean while claiming to be a Stoic. Going to a rich man's door is fine if reason requires it, as going to a shoemaker is, but flattery and kissing hands cost too much, since a good man acts for the sake of doing right. Affection must belong to a noble and happy man: Socrates loved his children as a free man, and Diogenes loved mankind as God's minister, at home anywhere, even among pirates. Antisthenes freed Diogenes by teaching him that only the use of appearances is his own. Epictetus scorns going abroad merely to learn syllogisms.","ideas":["Human life is a soldier's service done at the general's nod","Duty can take one to a rich man's door, but not to flattery","True affection is free and never makes a man a slave","Diogenes was free because nothing outside the will held him"],"terms":[{"t":"philostorgia (natural affection)","d":"Love for family and friends, which Epictetus accepts only when it leaves a man free and untroubled."},{"t":"chrēsis phantasiōn (use of appearances)","d":"The handling of impressions, which Antisthenes taught Diogenes is all that is truly his own."}]},{"i":448,"j":452,"loc":"Book III, chapter 24 (third part)","title":"Loving mortals, and serving as God's witness","summary":"The first exercise is to enjoy what one loves as one enjoys a cup that may break: remind yourself, even while kissing your child, that he is mortal, since wanting him when it is not given is like wanting a fig in winter. Words that name natural changes, like death or the falling of leaves, are not ill-omened, for death is a change to what is not now. The good man prays to God to keep him or release him as God wills, and to place him wherever he chooses. These thoughts should be ready day and night, so that every loss is expected and one lives in Gyara as in Gyara. The highest delight is to know oneself God's soldier and witness, sent to poverty or prison to show men where the good lies.","ideas":["Love what is mortal as something lent for a season","Death is a change into something the world now needs","Whatever post God assigns, the good man keeps it","Hardship makes a man a witness to what lies outside the will"],"terms":[{"t":"phantasia (appearance, impression)","d":"The impression of delight, which must be checked so that pleasure does not run unchecked."},{"t":"martys (witness)","d":"The role God gives the good man, to show by his life that good and evil are within."}]},{"i":453,"j":464,"loc":"Book III, chapters 25–26","title":"Renewing the contest; against fear of want","summary":"Those who have given up their purpose can renew the contest for happiness at once, without waiting four years as at Olympia, though they must not get the habit of yielding. Excusing a fault because one did it before is like a patient who says he has bathed before, forgetting the fever that followed. Against fear of want, Epictetus says that runaway slaves do not starve, that hunger leads only where fever leads, to death, and that taking humble work is not shameful, since only one's own acts are. The fearful student has practised syllogisms instead of learning to bear things, and really fears losing a life of servants and baths. God does not let a good man lack food or else sounds the retreat; Hercules and Ulysses relied on their opinions, and men should train against fear of death.","ideas":["The contest for happiness can always be begun again","Repeated faults strengthen the habit, as a bath strengthens a fever","Only what is one's own act can be shameful","The thing to fear is not death but the fear of death"],"terms":[{"t":"aischron (shameful)","d":"What deserves blame, which Epictetus limits to a man's own acts and not his poverty."}]},{"i":465,"j":472,"loc":"Book IV, chapter 1 (first part)","title":"Who is free? Consuls, lovers, and Caesar's friends","summary":"Book IV opens with freedom. The free man lives as he wishes, unhindered and uncompelled, so no bad man is free. A man twice consul who owns slaves may still be a slave if he acts under compulsion; even 'free' Nicopolitans swear by Caesar's fortune. The soldier Thrasonides, enslaved by a worthless girl, and caged lions and birds show what loss of freedom is, and Diogenes said freedom lies in readiness to die. The freed slave who seeks a patron, then rings, then office, then the senate keeps finding new slavery; his trouble, like everyone's, is failing to apply preconceptions to particular cases. The friend of Caesar admits he sleeps and dines worse, so neither kings nor their friends live as they wish.","ideas":["Freedom means living as one wishes, unhindered and uncompelled","Rank and birth do not free a man who acts under compulsion","The freed slave goes from one slavery to a finer one","All evils come from misapplying preconceptions to particular cases"],"terms":[{"t":"eleutheria (freedom)","d":"The condition of a man whose desires attain their end and whose aversions never meet what they avoid."},{"t":"prolēpsis (preconception)","d":"A general notion, such as that evil is harmful, which must be rightly applied to particular things."}]},{"i":473,"j":477,"loc":"Book IV, chapter 1 (second part)","title":"Many masters; what is in our power","summary":"Since freedom is great and self-governing, whoever flatters, says 'Master' from the heart, or laments is a slave, even with the twelve fasces before him. Our real masters are circumstances such as death, exile and loss of property, and through them whoever controls those things; so men worship the powerful as gods, from a false premise. What frees a man is knowledge of how to live, as skill in writing frees the writer. Under questioning, the speaker admits that body, estate, slaves and family are not in his power, but assent, impulse and the use of appearances are. He must treat his whole body like a loaded ass that a soldier may commandeer, and let it go without resistance.","ideas":["Anyone who controls what you desire or fear is your master","Men worship the powerful from a false premise about what benefits them","Assent and impulse cannot be compelled","The body is a borrowed ass, to be given up without a struggle"],"terms":[{"t":"ta eph' hēmin (things in our power)","d":"Assent, impulse, desire and the use of appearances, which no one else can hinder."},{"t":"hormē (impulse, movement to act)","d":"The soul's movement toward action, which remains one's own even when the body is hindered."}]},{"i":478,"j":483,"loc":"Book IV, chapter 1 (third part)","title":"Demolishing the inner acropolis; the festival of life","summary":"Once a man distinguishes what is his own from what is not, no one can frighten him; tyranny's fortress is pulled down by opinion, not by swords, when he gives up body, possessions and family as belonging to others. He then wills what God wills, even fever or the rack. As a cautious traveller waits for an official escort, the wise man attaches himself to God, not to Caesar. Everything, even oneself, was given by God, and life is a festival or spectacle to be left with thanks when it ends. Epictetus recommends practice beginning with a pot and a cup, recalls Diogenes instructing the master who bought him, and argues that Socrates and Helvidius did not fare badly, but their judges and murderers did.","ideas":["The tyrant's acropolis falls when opinions about externals are given up","Attaching oneself to God means willing what God wills","Life is a festival to be left gratefully when the giver ends it","Evil for a man is acting against his gentle, social nature"],"terms":[{"t":"akropolis (citadel)","d":"The image of the tyranny that externals hold within us, destroyed by right opinion."},{"t":"panēgyris (festival)","d":"The image of life as a feast or spectacle God invites us to for a time."}]},{"i":484,"j":490,"loc":"Book IV, chapter 1 (fourth part)","title":"Freedom tested: Diogenes and Socrates","summary":"Epictetus recapitulates: he who desires nothing that belongs to others is free, and the road is Cleanthes' prayer to be led by Zeus and destiny. He shames the student who, summoned by a tyrant to say something base, wants time to consider, although in the school he called life and death indifferent and betrayal evil. The rich who court old women for legacies and kiss slaves' hands for office are slaves, and Epictetus admits he is not yet fully free himself. Diogenes was free because everything only hung loosely on him, and Socrates, though he had wife and children, refused to seize Leon, would not escape, and saved himself by dying. Men should give back to God what he gave, since freedom comes from removing desire, not from fulfilling it.","ideas":["A philosopher who deliberates over base acts shows he never held his principles","Seekers of legacies and offices are slaves of slaves","Diogenes and Socrates show freedom with and without a family","Freedom comes by removing desire, not satisfying it"],"terms":[{"t":"adiaphora (things neither good nor bad)","d":"Life, death and prison, which the school taught are indifferent, unlike base acts."},{"t":"phantasia (imagination)","d":"The illusion that all good will arrive with the things men strive for."}]},{"i":491,"j":504,"loc":"Book IV, chapters 2–4","title":"Old friends, true exchanges, and loving leisure","summary":"Epictetus warns that a man cannot keep his old drinking friends and improve; he must choose between being liked as before and becoming better, as one cannot play both Thersites and Agamemnon. When something external is lost, count what was gained instead, since a small slip of attention can wreck everything, like a pilot turning slightly into the wind. Chapter 4 argues that desire for leisure, study and books enslaves a man as much as desire for office, because reading should serve a tranquil life. A man should judge his day by how he used impulse, desire and aversion, not by verses read. Crowds should be met as a festival and solitude as quiet, Cleanthes' prayer kept ready, and only he who refers his labour to his ruling faculty counted industrious.","ideas":["A man cannot improve while still doing what his old friends do","Every loss of an external may be an exchange for something better","Desire for leisure and books is also a form of slavery","Reading is preparation for life, and life is the contest"],"terms":[{"t":"euroia (tranquil flow of life)","d":"The smooth, unhindered life that reading and study should serve."},{"t":"katalēpsis (comprehension)","d":"Grasping an appearance firmly, which one should practise in fact, not only read about."}]},{"i":505,"j":509,"loc":"Book IV, chapter 5","title":"Against quarrelsomeness: the stamp of a man","summary":"The good man, following Socrates' example in Xenophon's Symposium, neither quarrels nor lets others quarrel, because no one controls another's ruling faculty. He thanks the man who reviles him for not striking him, and so on, since the offender never learned that injustice harms its doer. A man is judged by the stamp on his opinions as a coin is judged by Trajan's or Nero's head; without gentleness and fidelity he is an ass or a wild beast, whatever his shape. Opinions make the soul an impregnable city, and opinions about kingship and exile made Eteocles and Polynices enemies. If only the will is good or evil, there is nothing left to fight about, yet we are lions in the school and foxes outside.","ideas":["No one controls another person's ruling faculty","A man is known by the stamp of his opinions, not by his shape","Opinions alone make the soul impregnable","Strife comes from treating externals as good and evil"],"terms":[{"t":"prohairesis (will, moral choice)","d":"The faculty of choice, the only thing in which a man's good or evil lies."}]},{"i":510,"j":518,"loc":"Book IV, chapter 6","title":"Against those vexed at being pitied","summary":"A man complains that people pity him for poverty and lack of office. Epictetus replies that he can either try to convince everyone these are not evils, which even Zeus cannot do, or put on a false show of wealth; the only thing in his power is to convince himself, and he has not done so. He has studied syllogisms but not the theorems that free a man from grief, and his distress at being pitied makes him pitiable. Antisthenes' saying that it is royal to do well and be ill spoken of is set against him. Those who labour for power rightly gain power, while he who labours for right opinions should ask each morning only how he has used them.","ideas":["What others think of us is not in our power","Convincing oneself comes before convincing others","Each man gets more of what he has laboured for","A morning review should ask about right opinions, not flattery"],"terms":[{"t":"zōon logikon (rational animal)","d":"What a man is, as opposed to a body, a piece of property, or a subject of talk."}]},{"i":519,"j":525,"loc":"Book IV, chapter 7","title":"Freedom from fear of tyrants","summary":"The tyrant's guards frighten only those who care for what the swords can take; a child, or a man who wants to die, does not fear them. If madness and habit can make the Galilaeans fearless, reason can teach the same, since God made the universe free from hindrance and made man a rational part of it. The man with right opinions carries them into poverty, office or exile, and calls death only the body's return to its elements. Offices and provinces are dried figs and nuts scattered for children to fight over. He plays along at the tyrant's court until ordered to fetch Leon of Salamis, then refuses, and he knows he is not his corpse. He answers the charge that such talk undermines the laws: it only yields to the powerful what they are strong in, and keeps opinions as one's own.","ideas":["Swords frighten only those who value what they can take","Reason can teach what madness and habit teach others","Honours are figs and nuts that only children scramble for","A man is not his flesh but what makes use of it"],"terms":[{"t":"aphobia (freedom from fear)","d":"The state of one who neither fears what a tyrant can do nor desires what he can give."},{"t":"hēgemonikon (ruling faculty)","d":"The part that uses and tests all other faculties, which the powerful neglect."}]},{"i":526,"j":534,"loc":"Book IV, chapter 8","title":"Against rushing into the philosopher's cloak","summary":"Epictetus warns against praising or blaming acts whose opinions we do not know, and against judging a philosopher by cloak and long hair. When a carpenter uses an axe badly, people deny he is a carpenter, but when a philosopher behaves badly they blame philosophy. Philosophy's material is reason and its end reason in a right state. Euphrates long practised philosophy without being known as a philosopher, so that his faults endangered no one, and Socrates was content not to be recognised. The true Cynic, sent by Zeus as an example, must show his health before offering remedies. Those who grab the sceptre too soon are like seed forced by early warmth or an Adonis garden, bound to be frost-bitten.","ideas":["A philosopher is known by his opinions and acts, not his dress","A bad philosopher should lose the name, not discredit philosophy","Euphrates and Socrates philosophised without seeking recognition","Like seed, a philosopher must ripen hidden before showing fruit"],"terms":[{"t":"prolēpsis (preconception)","d":"The general notion of a philosopher, which people apply carelessly from external marks."},{"t":"gardens of Adonis","d":"Quickly forced plantings that wither, used for those who show themselves philosophers too early."}]},{"i":535,"j":545,"loc":"Book IV, chapters 9–10","title":"Recovering modesty; despising externals, valuing the will","summary":"To a man who has become shameless, Epictetus says that freedom from the desire for power, wealth or a beautiful woman is worth more than having them, since such desire is like a fever patient's thirst. He has lost modesty and good conduct by his own doing and can recover them by speaking to himself, since nothing is more tractable than the soul. Chapter 10 says all perplexity concerns externals; if good and evil lie in the will, whatever happens can be handled well. Epictetus would wish death to find him at a noble work or at least correcting himself, ready to tell God he never blamed him. Tranquillity has a price, as a consulship does, and Achilles mourning Patroclus shows the strongest and noblest wretched without right opinions.","ideas":["Not desiring wealth or power is worth more than possessing them","Ruin and help both come from within","No question of how things will turn out troubles one who values only the will","Tranquillity costs externals, just as office costs freedom"],"terms":[{"t":"aidōs (modesty)","d":"The sense of shame and decency the man lost through his own doing and can recover by himself."},{"t":"apatheia (freedom from passions)","d":"The untroubled state that must be bought by giving up concern for externals."}]},{"i":546,"j":554,"loc":"Book IV, chapter 11","title":"On purity of soul and body","summary":"Epictetus says that love of cleanliness distinguishes men from animals and comes from the gods, who are pure. The highest purity is the soul's, and the soul's impurity is nothing but bad judgments in its own acts, so it is cleansed by right opinions. The body should also be kept clean, with hands, water and strigils, both because this is human and so as not to offend companions. Socrates and Diogenes were pleasant to see, and a philosopher should not frighten people away by squalor. A young man who trims his hair at least has a notion of beauty that can be redirected to the rational part, but a man covered in filth cannot be led on, and no one should enter temples in such a state.","ideas":["The soul's impurity is its bad opinions","Cleanliness of body is part of being human and of respecting others","A philosopher's appearance should not repel people from philosophy","A love of beauty can be redirected to the rational faculty"],"terms":[{"t":"katharotēs (purity, cleanliness)","d":"Freedom from defilement, first of the soul by right opinions and then of the body."},{"t":"krimata (judgments)","d":"The soul's verdicts, whose badness is the only thing that makes it impure."}]},{"i":555,"j":564,"loc":"Book IV, chapters 12–13","title":"On attention; against telling one's secrets","summary":"Epictetus warns that relaxing attention for a day is not easily undone, because it builds a habit of inattention and of putting things off. No activity, not even play or song, is done better without attention. One must keep ready the principle that good and evil lie only in the will, obey God, and remember one's roles in life; faultlessness is impossible, but constant effort toward it is not. Chapter 13 explains why people tell their secrets in return for confidences and how Roman soldiers trap the unwary this way. Epictetus refuses such exchanges: a watertight cask should not trust a leaky one, and secrets require fidelity and right opinions, which are rare.","ideas":["Each lapse of attention makes the next one easier","Constant effort toward faultlessness is possible even if faultlessness is not","Hearing a secret does not oblige one to tell one's own","Confidence belongs to the faithful, the watertight cask"],"terms":[{"t":"prosochē (attention)","d":"Steady watchfulness over one's principles and conduct, which deferral steadily erodes."}]}]});